const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/dryer-repair/whirlpool-not-heating/";

const title = "Whirlpool Dryer Not Heating? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Whirlpool dryer won't heat? Usually a blown thermal fuse from a clogged vent (a fire risk), then the heating element or thermostat. Safe checks, when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Whirlpool dryer not heating?",
    a: "On a Whirlpool dryer that tumbles but won't heat, the signature cause is a blown thermal fuse — a one-time safety device that trips when a clogged exhaust vent makes the dryer overheat. After the fuse, the usual failed parts are the heating element and the thermal cut-off (high-limit thermostat). Whirlpool electric dryers may also show an AF (restricted airflow) message pointing at the same vent. On a gas Whirlpool, a weak igniter, failed gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor stops the burner. Clean the vent first; a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again until the airflow is fixed."
  },
  {
    q: "Do Whirlpool, Maytag, and Amana dryers share the same heating parts?",
    a: "Largely, yes — Whirlpool, Maytag, and Amana dryers are built on the same Whirlpool platform and share the heating architecture: heating element, thermal fuse, thermal cut-off and high-limit thermostat, and on gas models the same igniter, gas-valve coils, and flame sensor. Many Kenmore dryers (the ones whose model number starts with 110) are Whirlpool-built too and take the same parts. So the no-heat diagnosis is essentially identical across all four badges — which is why our techs treat them as one family. See our Maytag, Amana, and Kenmore not-heating guides for the brand-specific angles (warranty, model lookup)."
  },
  {
    q: "What does the AF code mean on a Whirlpool dryer?",
    a: "AF stands for restricted airflow. The dryer's control detected that air isn't moving through the exhaust the way it should — almost always a clogged lint filter, a blocked or crushed vent duct, or a plugged exterior flap. It's the same root cause behind most no-heat and blown-fuse calls. Clean the filter and the full vent, then touch any key to clear the message. If AF returns on a genuinely clear vent, the airflow sensor or the blower needs a look."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Whirlpool dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe, high-value step is yours: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent — the hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap included. That resolves most no-heat, AF, and blown-fuse calls and removes a real fire hazard. Also reset both halves of the 240V breaker on an electric Whirlpool; if one leg trips, the drum tumbles but the heat is dead. What you shouldn't do is open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, element, or thermostat, and never touch the gas line or gas valve on a gas model. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire hazard?",
    a: "Yes — the clogged vent that most often stops a Whirlpool from heating is also a leading fire cause. The U.S. Fire Administration reports roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. A Whirlpool running hot, showing AF, or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day — it fixes the heat and removes the hazard together."
  }
];

