<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A Whirlpool that won't heat almost always blew a thermal fuse.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Whirlpool dryer tumbles but won't heat, the signature cause is a blown thermal fuse — a one-time safety device that trips when a clogged exhaust vent makes the dryer overheat. After the fuse come the heating element and the thermal cut-off (high-limit thermostat); electric models may also show an AF restricted-airflow message pointing at the same vent. On a gas Whirlpool, it's a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or the flame sensor. Clean the filter and full vent first — a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again until the airflow is fixed. Still cold on a clear vent? It's a diagnostic. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your Whirlpool from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. A dryer running hot, showing AF, or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SHARED PLATFORM -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · One platform, four badges</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool, Maytag, Amana — and many Kenmores — share these parts.</h2>

    <p>Whirlpool, Maytag, and Amana dryers are built on the same Whirlpool platform and share the heating architecture: heating element, thermal fuse, thermal cut-off and high-limit thermostat, and on gas the same igniter, gas-valve coils, and flame sensor. Many <strong>Kenmore</strong> dryers — the ones whose model number starts with <strong>110</strong> — are Whirlpool-built and take the same parts. The no-heat diagnosis is essentially identical across all four, which is why we treat them as one family and stock the parts that fit all of them.</p>

    <p>The brand-specific angles differ (Maytag's longer parts warranty, Amana's value positioning, and the "who actually built my Kenmore" model lookup), and we cover those on the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/maytag-not-heating/">Maytag</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/amana-not-heating/">Amana</a>, and <a href="/services/dryer-repair/kenmore-not-heating/">Kenmore</a> not-heating guides. The diagnosis below applies to all of them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Whirlpool dryer stops heating</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Blown thermal fuse from a restricted vent (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes airflow, the dryer overheats, and the one-time <strong>thermal fuse</strong> trips and cuts the heat. It doesn't reset — it's replaced — and it only stays fixed once the vent that blew it is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>The element heats the air; when it burns open, the dryer tumbles cold with no code. Tested for continuity and replaced inside the cabinet — a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermal cut-off / high-limit thermostat</h3>
      <p>These protect against overheating and are usually replaced as a kit with the thermal fuse. Like the fuse, they trip because of a restricted vent, so the vent gets cleared at the same visit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>AF — restricted airflow message</h3>
      <p>An electric Whirlpool may flash AF when airflow drops. Same root cause as the blown fuse: clean the filter and vent, then touch any key to clear it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas Whirlpool, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve solenoid coils, or a bad flame sensor. An igniter that glows then goes out without the gas lighting points to the coils. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the flexible hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat, AF, and blown-fuse calls — and removes the fire hazard.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear an AF message.</strong> After cleaning the vent, touch any key (or power-cycle) to dismiss it.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix most Whirlpool no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, element, or thermostat, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? Time for a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the Whirlpool still won't heat, the fault is inside: an open thermal fuse and cut-off (plus the vent that blew them), a burned-out heating element, or on gas the igniter, valve coils, or flame sensor. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace the failed parts — usually the fuse and cut-off as a kit. These are among the more affordable dryer repairs, so a no-heat Whirlpool is well worth fixing. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide (all brands) and the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference. Same-platform siblings: <a href="/services/dryer-repair/maytag-not-heating/">Maytag</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/amana-not-heating/">Amana</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/kenmore-not-heating/">Kenmore</a>. A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/whirlpool-dryer-repair/">Whirlpool dryer repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a>, and <a href="/rancho-cucamonga/dryer-repair/">Rancho Cucamonga</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
